WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Unit cost, break-even, and setting your price.

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:08.060
This is the video for Module 2 of Pricing, Costing and Profit Planning.

3
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:14.520
Unit cost is what it costs you to make and sell one item. Here is the four-step method.

4
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:19.940
First, list every variable cost for one batch, with its quantity and current price.

5
00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.520
Second, add them and divide by the number of good units the batch gives.

6
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:37.480
Third, add up all monthly fixed costs, including your own wage, and divide by the units you realistically sell in a month. Use your average, not your best month.

7
00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.910
Fourth, add the two. That is your full cost per unit.

8
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:51.820
Burnt or broken items are not free. If a batch gives 48 good packs instead of 50, the good ones carry the cost of the bad ones.

9
00:00:53.070 --> 00:01:01.610
Ngozi's batch of flour, sugar, margarine, eggs, oil, pouches, gas and her helper's pay costs 47,000 naira.

10
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.870
Divided by 50 packs, her variable cost is 940 naira a pack.

11
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:20.960
Her monthly fixed costs, including her own wage of 60,000 naira, total 110,000 naira. Shared over 200 packs, that is 550 naira a pack.

12
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:31.850
So each pack really costs 1,490 naira. Her old price of 1,500 naira leaves only 10 naira of profit.

13
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:49.320
Each sale first pays its own variable cost. What is left is the contribution margin. At 1,500 naira, Ngozi's contribution is 1,500 minus 940, which is 560 naira a pack.

14
00:01:50.570 --> 00:02:00.570
Break-even is fixed costs divided by contribution. 110,000 divided by 560 is 196.4.

15
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:12.530
Always round up. Ngozi must sell 197 packs a month just to cover her costs and her wage. Only the 198th pack gives profit.

16
00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:25.980
If she expects to sell 250 packs, her margin of safety is 53 packs, about 21 percent. Below about 10 percent, one bad week could push the month into a loss.

17
00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:35.100
Use break-even to test a price. At 1,500 naira, Ngozi needs 197 packs a month.

18
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:47.750
At 1,700 naira, her contribution becomes 760 naira, and she needs only 145 packs. That is 52 fewer packs every month.

19
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:56.200
But never price below your variable cost. For Ngozi, any price under 940 naira loses money on every pack.

20
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:11.040
Markup is profit as a percentage of cost. A 30 percent markup on 1,490 naira gives 1,937 naira. But that is only about a 23 percent margin.

21
00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:25.200
Margin is profit as a percentage of price. For a 30 percent margin, divide 1,490 by 0.70. That gives about 2,130 naira.

22
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:35.430
The market says similar packs sell for up to about 2,000 naira, so Ngozi chooses 1,700 naira for now and works to raise volume.

23
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.580
Here is how the mistake happens.

24
00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:49.600
Funke's jar costs 2,000 naira, and she priced it at 3,000 naira. That is a 50 percent markup, but only a 33 percent margin.

25
00:03:50.850 --> 00:04:03.710
A true 50 percent margin would need 4,000 naira. That is too high for her market, so she settles on 3,500 naira, about 43 percent. Now it is a conscious choice.

26
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:19.850
Shops need their own profit, so they buy at a lower price. If Ngozi sells to a shop at 1,400 naira, she still makes 460 naira contribution, and the shop resells at 1,700 naira.

27
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.470
Keep wholesale above variable cost, and set a minimum order so the lower price is earned by volume.

28
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:43.890
To remember. Full cost is variable plus fixed cost per unit. Break-even is fixed costs divided by contribution, rounded up. A small price rise can cut it sharply. Use the margin formula, not a markup, for a target margin.

29
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.080
And keep wholesale prices above variable cost.

30
00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:57.890
Now cost one unit of your main product, find your break-even point and margin of safety, and price it with both markup and margin before you choose.

31
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.180
Continue with the lessons below.
